course: dictionary
lesson: 7577
title: Pressurise: push hard, or keep air at a set level
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Pressurise has two meanings. The first is to push someone hard to do something, often by persuading them again and again. «Ravi's friends kept pressurising him to try the dare, but he said no.» They kept pushing until he gave in or stood firm.
explain: The second meaning is about air. To pressurise something is to keep the air inside it at a set level, usually higher than the air outside. «Astronauts need pressurised suits, because in space there is no air pressing on the outside of their bodies.» The suit keeps air pressed in so the astronaut can breathe.
explain: Clues in the sentence tell you which meaning fits. Ask: is this about people, or about a suit, plane or can? If it is about people, pressurise means push or pressure. If it is about a machine or container, it means keeping the air at a set level. Then try your meaning in the sentence to check it.
fact: Pressurise means to try to make someone do something by pushing or persuading them hard.
fact: Pressurise also means to keep the air pressure inside an aeroplane, spacesuit or container at a set level, usually higher than outside.
fact: Aeroplane cabins are pressurised so people can breathe high up, and aerosol cans are pressurised too.
fact: It is fine to say no if friends pressurise you to do something you don't want to do.
fact: In American English the spelling is pressurize.
exercise: «Ravi's friends kept pressurising him to try the dare.» What does pressurising mean here? | *Pushing him hard to do it | Giving him a present | Looking after him carefully | Carrying him home
exercise: «Astronauts need pressurised suits.» What does pressurised suggest about the suit? | It is painted bright green | It is made of wet paper | *It has air kept at a set level inside | It is very old and dusty
exercise: What is the best thing to do if friends keep pressurising you to do something you don't want to do? | *Say no, because it is fine to say no | Shout at everyone in the playground | Give in just to stop them | Hide in the toilets for the rest of the day
test: «The aeroplane cabin is pressurised so people can breathe high up.» What does pressurised mean here? | Painted red | *Kept with air at a set level | Filled with sand | Covered in ice
test: Jade's mum says, «Don't let anyone pressurise you into buying things.» What does pressurise mean? | To sing loudly | To clean the house | To buy things in a shop | *To push someone hard to do something
exam: Which sentence uses pressurise to mean push someone hard? | *"Her coach kept pressurising the team to train harder." | "The aeroplane cabin is pressurised." | "The spacesuit was pressurised." | "The aerosol can was pressurised."
